The initial configured data should cover the College of Technology.

The design must also allow other colleges to be added in the future.

## Module 4: Task Management

The Coordinator can:

- Create tasks.
- Edit tasks.
- View tasks.
- Publish tasks.
- Cancel tasks.
- Complete tasks.
- Archive tasks.
- Assign categories.
- Set visibility.
- Set student capacity.
- Set required hours.
- Define application dates.
- Define activity dates.
- Set locations.
- Create multiple task sessions.
- Assign target sections.
- Assign selected students.

### Task fields

Tasks should support:

- College
- Category
- Creator
- Title
- Description
- Location
- Visibility
- Capacity
- Required hours
- Application start
- Application deadline
- Activity start
- Activity end
- Status

### Task visibility

Three visibility options:

1. all_students
2. specific_sections
3. selected_students

For specific_sections, use the task_target_sections table.

For selected_students, use the task_target_students table.

The application must enforce visibility and eligibility through backend authorization logic, not just frontend filtering.

### Task statuses

Use:

- draft
- published
- ongoing
- completed
- cancelled
- archived

### Task validation

- Capacity must be greater than zero when provided.
- Required hours must be nonnegative when provided.
- Application deadline must be after application start when both exist.
- Activity end must be after activity start when both exist.
- A published task must meet required publication validation.
- Prevent unauthorized task editing.
- Capacity must be protected against simultaneous application requests.

## Module 5: Task Sessions

A task can have multiple sessions.

Each session should support:

- Session title
- Session date
- Start time
- End time
- Location

Validate that the end time is later than the start time.

Attendance records must reference valid task sessions.

## Module 6: Task Application Management

Students can apply for eligible tasks.

The Coordinator reviews the submitted applications.

Application statuses:

- pending
- approved
- rejected
- withdrawn
- cancelled

Rules:

- A student cannot submit duplicate applications for the same task.
- Application capacity must be respected.
- Students must be eligible for the task.
- Pending or rejected applications do not count as approved participants.
- Withdrawn applications must remain historically recorded.
- Coordinator decisions must be stored.
- Review remarks should be recorded where provided.
- Capacity handling must use safe database transactions and appropriate row locking.

## Module 7: Attendance and Service Hour Management

This is a critical module.

Attendance must be connected to the approved task application and valid task session.

Attendance includes:

- Attendance date
- Check-in time
- Check-out time
- Recorded hours
- Verified hours
- Verification status
- Verifying Coordinator
- Verification timestamp
- Remarks

Attendance verification statuses:

- pending
- verified
- rejected

Rules:

- Check-out must be later than check-in when both exist.
- Recorded hours must not be negative.
- Verified hours must not be negative.
- Attendance must reference a valid application and task session.
- A student must not be able to verify their own attendance.
- Only authorized Coordinator actions can verify attendance.
- Attendance should not be silently overwritten.

## Service hour formula

The official credited service-hour calculation is:

Total Credited RSS Hours =
SUM(verified attendance hours)
+
SUM(approved adjustment hours)

Only verified attendance hours count.

Only approved adjustment hours count.

Pending and rejected attendance must not contribute.

Pending and rejected adjustments must not contribute.

The dashboard must distinguish recorded, pending, rejected, and verified hours.

## Module 8: Service Hour Adjustments

Allow documented hour corrections.

An adjustment should contain:

- Application
- Requesting user
- Approving user where applicable
- Adjustment hours
- Reason
- Status
- Approval timestamp

Adjustment hours may be positive or negative.

The approved design does not yet specify the complete allowed status vocabulary. Do not silently invent a final status constraint. Present the proposed values for review before enforcing them.

Only approved adjustments count in the official calculation.

Keep the adjustment history.

## Module 9: Accomplishment Management

After service participation, students document their accomplishments.

The student creates an accomplishment and submits a version for Coordinator review.

Accomplishment statuses:

- draft
- submitted
- revision_required
- approved
- rejected

The Coordinator can:

- View submissions.
- Read descriptions.
- View activity dates.
- View student remarks.
- View evidence images.
- Approve.
- Request revision.
- Reject.
- Add review remarks.
- View previous versions.

An accomplishment belongs to one task application.

The application-to-accomplishment relationship is one-to-zero-or-one.

## Module 10: Accomplishment Submission Versioning

Every submitted version must be preserved.

A student may submit a new version after a revision request.

The system must not overwrite the previous submitted version.

Maintain:

- Accomplishment
- Submitted by
- Version number
- Activity date
- Description
- Student remarks
- Submission timestamp
- Review history
- Evidence associated with the specific submission version

Use a unique version number per accomplishment.

Do not invent additional submission statuses. The exact approved status values for accomplishment_submissions still need confirmation.

## Module 11: Evidence Image Management

Students can upload multiple photographs for a submission.

Image categories:

- before
- during
- after
- other

Each evidence record should store:

- Original filename
- Protected storage path
- MIME type
- File size
- Image category
- Caption
- Display order
- Uploader
- Associated submission

Requirements:

- Validate file type.
- Validate size.
- Use protected storage.
- Do not trust client-provided filenames.
- Prevent executable files from being uploaded as images.
- Store files outside public unrestricted access.
- Provide authorized viewing.
- Preserve evidence tied to historical submissions.
- Allow an appropriate gallery interface.
- Preserve category and display order.

Store the file itself in protected storage. PostgreSQL stores file metadata and the storage path.

## Module 12: RSS Report Generation

This module generates the official RSS documents.

It must support:

1. Individual RSS reports
2. Section RSS reports

### Individual report

The Coordinator selects the exact approved accomplishment submission.

The report must link to that exact approved submission.

Do not simply use the latest version.

### Section report

The Coordinator selects the appropriate section and academic year.

The system gathers eligible approved submissions through report_items.

The section report must preserve which submissions were included.

### Report statuses

Use:

- generated
- approved
- archived

### Report template management

